# ---- Terraform ------------------------------------------------------------
# State contains the generated RDS master password in plaintext. It must never
# be committed. Production would use an encrypted S3 backend with DynamoDB
# locking instead of local state - see README §Production deltas.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup

# NOTE: .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins the exact
# provider versions so every clone resolves identically, and it contains no
# secrets. HashiCorp recommends committing it.
.terraform/
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Variable files may carry an email address or a domain.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!terraform.tfvars.example

# Saved plans embed a full copy of tfstate, so they contain every sensitive
# value state does - including the generated RDS master password in plaintext.
# Match by CONTENT-ish name, not by exact filename: an earlier version of this
# file listed only `tfplan` and `*.tfplan`, and plans saved as `fixplan` and
# `snsplan` were committed to a public repository as a result.
# CONVENTION: always save plans as `terraform plan -out=tfplan`. Name-based
# ignore rules cannot anticipate every filename - `fixplan` and `snsplan`
# escaped a rule listing only `tfplan`, and `redeploy` later escaped `*plan`
# too. The patterns below are a safety net, NOT the primary control. The
# primary control is using one filename, every time.
tfplan
*.tfplan
*plan
*plan.bin
redeploy*
destroyplan*
*.tfstate.backup
!*.tf
!*.tfvars.example

override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# ---- Local ----------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---- Course material ------------------------------------------------------
# The project brief is someone else's document. Kept locally, not published.
SAA - Projects Ideas.pdf
*.pdf

# baseline captured at runtime, not committed
docs/.failover-baseline.txt
